  "body": "The algorithm is really complex, so I might be reading it incorrectly.\r\n\r\nConsider markup like this:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<div id=\"popover-1\" popover>Popover 1 <button popovertarget=\"popover-2\">Open popover 2</button></div>\r\n<div id=\"popover-2\" popover>Popover 2</div>\r\n<button popovertarget=\"popover-1\">Open popover 1</button>\r\n<button popovertarget=\"popover-2\">Open popover 2</button>\r\n```\r\n\r\n[Demo](https://jsbin.com/junihob/3/edit?html,output)\r\n\r\n1. Open popover 1\r\n2. Open popover 2 using the last button in the source (the one that _isn't_ inside the other popover)\r\n\r\nWhen the spec is deciding which popovers to close, it [looks at all buttons on the page](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/popover.html#topmost-popover-ancestor:~:text=For%20each%20button%20invoker%20that%20is%20a%20descendant%20of%20newPopover%27s%20root%2C%20in%20tree%20order%3A) rather than the button that was used. This doesn't make sense to me, and doesn't match Chrome's implementation.\r\n\r\nhttps://youtu.be/VO1C5YPITsk - here's a readthrough of the spec.\r\n\r\nI'm not sure what the actual intended behaviour is (the spec is complicated and buggy). [The note mentions a \"tree of popovers\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/popover.html#topmost-popover-ancestor:~:text=ways%2C%20creating%20a-,tree%20of%20popovers,-.%20There%20are%20two), but it seems like only a list is needed? Is there a case where two open popovers have the same parent?\r\n\r\nI also feel we should match buttons up to closest popovers early. The current spec looks at the position and state of the button when a new popover is opening, but that feels like the wrong time to check. Eg, a button must be enabled when it's used to open a popup. It doesn't matter if it's disabled when later trying to determine the popover tree. In fact, the button may have been later removed.",
